.\" Manual page for the packaged dnscrypt-proxy as this package installs and configures it.
.TH DNSCRYPT\-PROXY 8 "2026-09-27" "mosdns-router 0.1.0" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
dnscrypt\-proxy \- the foreign resolver, on loopback only
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B dnscrypt\-proxy
.B \-config
.I /etc/mosdns/dnscrypt-proxy.toml
.SH DESCRIPTION
This is the page for
.BR dnscrypt\-proxy (8)
as THIS PACKAGE builds, configures and runs it: dnscrypt\-proxy 2.1.18, compiled
from a checksummed source archive with cgo disabled, installed at
.IR /usr/lib/mosdns\-router/dnscrypt\-proxy ,
configured by
.IR /etc/mosdns/dnscrypt\-proxy.toml ,
and started by
.BR dnscrypt\-proxy.service (5).
The program's own documentation is in the configuration file's comments and in its
upstream release notes; what is written here is the configuration this package
ships and the properties of the unit that runs it.
.SH THE SHIPPED CONFIGURATION
.TP
.B listen_addresses
.BR 127.0.0.1:15353 ,
and nothing else. Never port 53, so this resolver is neither reachable from
off\-host nor mistakable for the system resolver. dnscrypt\-proxy registers a UDP
and a TCP listener for every listen address, so nothing here narrows it to one
transport; the router's foreign forward uses TCP.
.TP
.B ignore_system_dns
.BR true .
The host resolver list is what a DHCP lease published, and this router does not
control it, so a foreign query must never be answered by the network the user is
leaving. The bootstrap resolvers below resolve DNSCrypt provider names and never
receive an ordinary user query.
.TP
.B cache
.BR false .
The foreign cache belongs to the router, which is the only component that knows
when an answer has been superseded; a second cache here would answer from a copy
nothing can invalidate, and a query log would write the user's names to disk.
.TP
.B doh_servers , odoh_servers
Both
.BR false ,
so no stamp may name a DoH or an ODoH endpoint: the program would drop such a stamp
and keep serving with fewer resolvers than the file lists, without saying so.
.TP
.B require_dnssec , require_nolog
Both
.BR true ,
so a resolver that cannot promise DNSSEC and no\-log is refused rather than used.
.TP
.B netprobe_address
.BR 9.9.9.9:443 .
A reachability probe over TCP 443, so a captive portal or a blackholed network is
reported before the router claims to resolve anything. It is pinned rather than
defaulted to the first bootstrap resolver, which would probe plain DNS on port 53
where a portal happily answers.
.TP
.B [static.*]
The stamps. Each is complete: it carries the 32\-byte Ed25519 provider public key
the resolver's certificate is signed with, so the address and the key it has to
prove cannot be separated. Nothing in the file is a bare resolver IP, because an
unauthenticated address is exactly what an on\-path party substitutes.
.SH CHANGING IT
The resolver document is GENERATED:
.BR "mosdns\-cdnctl render" (1)
rewrites it from the policy, so a custom stamp set is an edit that a render will
overwrite. Two honest ways to have a different provider: keep a copy of the
document and restore it after a render, or change the renderer. Editing it in
place and then rendering is a configuration nobody can explain.
.PP
Any change needs a restart, because the unit reads the file once at start:
.PP
.RS
.nf
sudo systemctl restart dnscrypt\-proxy
.fi
.RE
.PP
Check the document before restarting it. The program validates a document and exits
without binding a socket, which makes it the one command that can tell an operator
their edit parses before the router loses its foreign branch:
.PP
.RS
.nf
/usr/lib/mosdns\-router/dnscrypt\-proxy \-check \-config /etc/mosdns/dnscrypt\-proxy.toml
.fi
.RE
.SH THE UNIT
It runs as
.BR dnscrypt\-proxy ,
which shares no state with either mosdns identity and so has its own group and its
own umask. It binds an unprivileged port and runs no privileged operation, so its
capability bounding set is emptied rather than inherited. Its address\-family list
includes
.BR AF_NETLINK ,
and that is not a detail: dnscrypt\-proxy 2.1.18 enumerates the host's interfaces in
its network\-change monitor, Go's
.I net.Interfaces
opens a netlink socket, and without the family the fingerprint loses every
interface and a change of network is missed silently.
.PP
.B network.target
is an ordering edge and nothing else. This resolver must start on a machine whose
network is not up, because it resolves provider names through pinned bootstrap
resolvers rather than through the system resolver.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR "mosdns\-cdnctl" (1),
.BR mosdns\-router (8),
.BR systemd.unit (5),
.BR tmpfiles.d (5)
